# SL Politics — environment configuration (DEVELOPMENT defaults).
#
# Copy to `.env` and adjust. `.env` is gitignored; never commit real values.
# Nothing here has a secret in it today — SL Politics reads only public government
# pages and needs no credentials. This file exists so that when a deployment
# does need one (a database password), there is an obvious place for it that
# is already excluded from version control.
#
# THREE ENVIRONMENTS, THREE FILES. Every value below is a safe LOCAL default —
# SQLite instead of Postgres, localhost CORS, every sync schedule off. For the
# other two environments, see:
#
#   .env.staging.example      a real deployment, a real (but non-production)
#                              database, safe to point automatic sync at
#   .env.production.example   the live site
#
# Both are short DIFFS against this file, not full restatements — they change
# only what actually needs to differ (origins, DATABASE_URL, whether sync runs)
# and point back here for what every variable means and why it defaults the
# way it does. Duplicating 150 lines of reasoning three times would be three
# copies to keep in sync and, inevitably, three copies that drift.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- build

# The public origin the site is served from. REQUIRED to build: the build
# fails rather than guessing.
#
# Every prerendered page carries an absolute <link rel="canonical"> and every
# sitemap entry is an absolute URL. Both must name the production host. Derived
# from window.location this works in a browser but bakes "http://localhost:5173"
# into 1,600 static files, telling search engines the real copy of every page
# lives on a machine they cannot reach — so there is no safe default.
VITE_SITE_ORIGIN=https://javora.lk

# CSP violation reporting (L-8, docs/security-audit-followup-2026-09-04.md).
# Unset by default — a policy violation in production is otherwise invisible,
# but this is optional and never required to build. When set, the generated
# CSP (scripts/prerender.mjs, dist/csp.txt, dist/_headers) gains both
# `report-uri` and `report-to` directives, plus a matching
# `Reporting-Endpoints` header — see scripts/csp.mjs. Point it at wherever
# your deployment actually collects CSP reports (a small logging endpoint, a
# third-party report collector); this repository does not provide one.
# CSP_REPORT_URI=https://reports.javora.lk/csp

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- database

# Where the canonical SQLite database lives.
JAVORA_DB=.data/javora.db

# PostgreSQL connection string.
#
# TESTED against a real PostgreSQL 16 server, twice over:
#   - server/db/postgres.ts applied every migration file unchanged,
#     scripts/migrate-to-postgres.mjs copied all 3,230 rows across 29 tables
#     in one transaction, and scripts/validate-postgres-migration.mjs
#     confirmed every row byte-identical to the SQLite source.
#   - server/api/server.ts's five read endpoints (health, people, a single
#     person, search, facets) were run live against that migrated data
#     through a real connection pool, including two real cross-backend bugs
#     found and fixed this way (Postgres folding unquoted camelCase SQL
#     aliases to lowercase, and node-postgres returning COUNT(*) as a string)
#     — see server/api/queries.postgres.test.ts, which guards against both.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT MEAN. Setting DATABASE_URL switches those FIVE
# read endpoints (and only those) onto PostgreSQL, with pooling, when the API
# is started. It does NOT affect the sync worker or the CLI, and it does NOT
# affect the API's own /api/status, /api/sources or /api/government/current —
# all of those still read `JAVORA_DB` (SQLite) through the synchronous
# `Database` interface in server/db/database.ts, because CanonicalStore
# (server/db/store.ts) and its ~150 other call sites remain synchronous.
# Converting those is a separate, larger undertaking — see
# server/db/postgres.ts's header comment for exactly what it would take.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@localhost:5432/javora

# For server/api/queries.postgres.test.ts's cross-backend parity suite only —
# a THROWAWAY database (its schema is dropped and rebuilt on every run).
# Never point this at anything holding real data. Unset: that test file is
# skipped, which is also what happens in ordinary `npm test`.
# DATABASE_URL_TEST=postgres://postgres:password@localhost:5432/javora_test

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------- api

JAVORA_API_PORT=4000

# Browser origins allowed to call the API. Comma-separated, no wildcards —
# the API echoes an origin only when it appears on this list.
JAVORA_CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000,http://localhost:5173

# Milliseconds before a single database query is abandoned and the request
# fails with 500, rather than holding a connection (and the client) open
# indefinitely against a stuck query or an exhausted connection pool.
API_QUERY_TIMEOUT_MS=8000

# A per-IP fixed-window rate limit, held in PROCESS MEMORY. Running more than
# one API instance means each instance enforces this independently — the
# effective limit across N instances is N times what is set here, not a
# shared budget. A single-instance deployment (what this project ships) does
# not have that gap; a horizontally-scaled one would need a shared limiter
# (Redis, a gateway) instead of raising these numbers.
API_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=60000
API_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=120

# Response caching for the two read-heavy endpoints worth caching — a small
# in-process TTL memoizer (server/api/cache.ts), not a cache server; measured
# to cut a repeat request from ~50ms to ~4ms under load. The two numbers are
# DELIBERATELY different by 20x, and that gap is load-bearing, not arbitrary:
#
#   API_GOVERNMENT_CACHE_TTL_MS   who currently holds office. The one fact
#                                 where staleness costs the most — kept SHORT
#                                 on purpose. cache.test.ts fails the build if
#                                 this is ever configured longer than the
#                                 facets TTL below.
#   API_FACETS_CACHE_TTL_MS       party/district/role COUNTS. Nobody makes a
#                                 decision based on a count being a few
#                                 minutes old, so this can safely be much
#                                 longer.
#
# /api/people and /api/search are NOT cached: their query space is
# effectively unbounded (every filter combination and search term is a
# different cache key), and both already measured at 1.8–4.3ms against the
# real database — there is nothing slow here for a cache to fix.
API_GOVERNMENT_CACHE_TTL_MS=15000
API_FACETS_CACHE_TTL_MS=300000

# Whether to read the client's rate-limit identity from X-Forwarded-For, and
# from whom. Empty by default: trusting that header with no reverse proxy in
# front of the API lets any client simply set their own rate-limit identity by
# sending it directly — and taking the WRONG (left-most, client-controlled)
# entry from it is its own bug even behind a real proxy.
#
# JAVORA_TRUSTED_PROXIES is a comma-separated CIDR/address allowlist
# (e.g. "127.0.0.1/32,10.0.0.0/8"). X-Forwarded-For is honoured only when
# `req.socket.remoteAddress` — the actual TCP peer — is inside this list, and
# then only the RIGHT-MOST entry not itself in the list is trusted as the
# client's real address (server/api/trustedProxies.ts). Set it only once a
# real reverse proxy sits in front of the API and OVERWRITES (not appends to)
# any client-supplied X-Forwarded-For — deploy/nginx-api.conf does this.
#
# JAVORA_TRUST_PROXY (the old boolean) still SILENCES nothing if left set: it
# is read only to log a deprecation warning and no longer grants any trust —
# see server/api/server.ts.
JAVORA_TRUSTED_PROXIES=

# Hard cap on the in-memory rate-limit bucket map — the map degrades (evicts
# the oldest-expiring entry) rather than growing without bound once a flood of
# distinct client addresses reaches this size.
API_RATE_BUCKET_CAP=50000

# ------------------------------------------------------------- corrections
#
# POST /api/corrections is the ONE endpoint that writes (server/api/
# corrections.ts, designed in docs/corrections-security-design.md). It appends
# a row to `correction_report` with review_status='open' and can reach no
# other table; publishing an approved correction stays a separate, human
# triggered action.
#
#   JAVORA_CORRECTIONS=off        disable the endpoint. It then answers 503
#                                 and the form says plainly that submissions
#                                 are not connected — which is the honest
#                                 answer, and better than accepting reports
#                                 that would not be stored.
#   JAVORA_CORRECTION_HASH_SALT   salts `correction_report.submitter_ip_hash`,
#                                 the per-reporter token a moderator uses to
#                                 spot "twelve reports from one network this
#                                 hour". SET THIS to a long random string in
#                                 any real deployment: unset, a fresh salt is
#                                 generated per process, so the signal stops
#                                 comparing across a restart. Never reuse it
#                                 as a secret for anything else.
#
# The rate limits are deliberately far tighter than the read API's, and on
# their own hour-long window. A real reporter files one correction at a time,
# by hand, occasionally. The GLOBAL ceiling is the one that matters most: a
# per-IP limit alone is defeated by spreading submissions across a pool of
# addresses, and the failure mode this feature actually has to survive is not
# a corrupted database — it is a queue flooded past the point where a human
# can review it carefully.
JAVORA_CORRECTIONS=
JAVORA_CORRECTION_HASH_SALT=
API_CORRECTION_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MS=3600000
API_CORRECTION_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=5
API_CORRECTION_GLOBAL_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=200
# Body cap, enforced while reading, before JSON.parse. Keep in step with
# deploy/nginx-api.conf's client_max_body_size.
API_CORRECTION_MAX_BODY_BYTES=8192

# -------------------------------------------------------------- logging
#
# The API and worker both write ONE JSON OBJECT PER LINE to stdout (events:
# request served, sync started/finished, shutdown) and stderr (errors — see
# `logError` in server/api/server.ts). There is no LOG_LEVEL here because
# there is nothing to filter: every line is already the granularity an
# operator would want (one per request, one per sync run), not a stream of
# debug noise a level would need to silence. Nothing logged here is more
# sensitive than what the health endpoint already discloses — no connection
# string, no credential, no stack trace reaches a client, but stack traces
# DO go to this stream on a genuine server error (see `logError`), so treat
# captured logs with the same care as any other operational data.
#
# Capture it with whatever the deployment already runs: `docker logs`, a
# systemd unit's journal, or a platform's own log capture (Fly.io, Render,
# Railway all do this with zero configuration for a process that logs to
# stdout/stderr, which is why nothing here needs a file path or a log
# shipper's address).

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- frontend

# Point the React app at the API. LEAVE UNSET to use the bundled dataset,
# which needs no server running. Setting it switches the data source.
#
# It also decides whether the corrections form can submit at all: read at
# BUILD time, so a static bundle built without it has no API origin to post
# to and says so on the page rather than pretending. Setting it also adds the
# origin to the site's CSP connect-src (scripts/csp.mjs) — a build that points
# the app at an API the CSP does not allow produces a form that fails silently
# in the browser.
# VITE_API_URL=http://localhost:4000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- scheduler
#
# Minutes between automatic checks of each source. 0 DISABLES the schedule,
# and 0 is the default for every source: automatically retrieving a
# government website should be a deliberate decision by whoever runs the
# deployment, not a side effect of installing the software.
#
# A schedule only runs when the interval is positive AND a connector exists.
# Today that is true for S001 (Parliament) and S006 (Cabinet Office).
#
# RECOMMENDED VALUES, when enabled — see server/scheduler/schedule.ts for the
# full reasoning:
#   PARLIAMENT_SYNC_INTERVAL=360   (6h)  membership moves rarely; this is a
#                                        short leash anyway, not a floor.
#   CABINET_SYNC_INTERVAL=180      (3h)  twice as often as Parliament — a
#                                        reshuffle is rarer but far more
#                                        consequential to reflect quickly.

# Parliament of Sri Lanka — membership changes rarely outside an election.
PARLIAMENT_SYNC_INTERVAL=0
PARLIAMENT_REQUEST_DELAY_MS=500
# Also fetch each member's profile page (225 extra requests per run).
PARLIAMENT_SYNC_PROFILES=false

# Cabinet Office — the source behind the Current Government page.
CABINET_SYNC_INTERVAL=0
CABINET_REQUEST_DELAY_MS=1000

# ---- Fetch reliability (applies to every source) --------------------------
#
# Timeout and retry for the FETCH step. Tested live: retried a genuinely
# unreachable source through all configured attempts, and recovered from a
# single simulated transient failure without failing the run.
SYNC_FETCH_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
SYNC_RETRY_ATTEMPTS=3
SYNC_RETRY_BACKOFF_MS=2000

# A run recorded as started but never finished, older than this, is treated
# as abandoned by a worker process that no longer exists (a crash, a
# mid-run redeploy) rather than a permanent lock on that source.
SYNC_MAX_RUN_MINUTES=30

# ---- Not connected -------------------------------------------------------
#
# No connector is implemented for the sources below, so these intervals are
# inert: the worker skips any source without a connector rather than
# pretending a schedule exists. Setting them to a positive number changes
# nothing until a connector is written.
#
#   S002  Election Commission     2024 results are per-district PDFs; no PDF
#                                 text extraction exists in this project.
#   S005  Government Gazette      the published archive URL returns 404.
#   S003  Presidential Secretariat  every page returns 403 behind a bot check.

ELECTION_SYNC_INTERVAL=0
ELECTION_REQUEST_DELAY_MS=1000

GAZETTE_SYNC_INTERVAL=0
GAZETTE_REQUEST_DELAY_MS=1000

PRESIDENTIAL_SYNC_INTERVAL=0
PRESIDENTIAL_REQUEST_DELAY_MS=1000

PM_SYNC_INTERVAL=0
PM_REQUEST_DELAY_MS=1000
